/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The true value is the quantity as it really is: the distance between two pegs, the angle between two lines.
   - e is the error, in the same unit as the measurement; x is the observed value; X is the true or accepted value.
   - A tape reads 100.05 m on a line whose true length is 100.00 m.
   - c is the correction, added to the observed value; e is the error.
   - Sign matters more than anything else here.
   - Report only the significant figures the instrument supports.
   - Gross errors, or blunders, are mistakes: reading 4.62 as 4.92, booking a backsight in the foresight column, dropping a whole 30 m tape length.
   - Systematic errors repeat with the same sign and about the same size under the same conditions.
   - Because the sign and size repeat, averaging does not remove a systematic error.
   - Random errors are the small scatter in any reading: the bubble never sits in exactly the same place, the pull on the tape is not steady, the mark is estimated to the nearest half millimetre.
*/
