/* book exam traps: Correction is the negative of the error. Adding the error instead of subtracting it doubles the mistake — a favourite one-mark deduction. | Low scatter is not proof of correctness: in the worked example the readings are tight but the mean is 30 mm high. | Averaging kills random error, not systematic error. More readings shrink s/√n and leave the bias untouched. | Dividing Σdᵢ² by n instead of n − 1 makes s too small; and the squares are in m², so the standard deviation comes back in m. | A blunder is rejected and re-measured, never averaged in. */
